---

<section id="motivation" class="py-16 sm:py-20 px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 max-w-7xl mx-auto">
  <div class="max-w-3xl mb-10">
    <h2 class="text-3xl sm:text-5xl font-bold tracking-tight uppercase leading-none">
      All the benefits of Protobuf.
      <br />
      <span class="font-serif italic font-normal normal-case text-[#FF4F18]">
        Faster, inspectable, and spliceable.
      </span>
    </h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-lg sm:text-xl text-ink/80 font-medium">
      You shouldn't have to give up <code>.proto</code> files or switch to pointer-heavy zero-copy
      formats just to get fast serialization and readable packets.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="border-t-2 border-ink divide-y-2 divide-ink/20">
    <!-- 1. FASTER -->
    <div class="py-8 sm:py-10 grid lg:grid-cols-12 gap-6 lg:gap-8 items-center">
      <div class="lg:col-span-8 space-y-3">
        <h3 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold uppercase tracking-tight leading-snug">
          No byte-by-byte varint loops. No two-pass encoding.
        </h3>
        <p class="text-base sm:text-lg text-ink/90 leading-relaxed max-w-[60ch]">
          Protobuf's{' '}
          <a
            href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/LEB128"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener"
            class="doc-link"
          >
            LEB128
          </a>{' '}
          varints check a continuation bit on every byte, and its length-prefixed sub-messages force
          encoders to walk the struct tree twice (once to compute sizes, once to write bytes).
          OmniBuf puts the integer byte width in the first byte (
          <a
            href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Find_first_set"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener"
            class="doc-link"
          >
            <code>TZCNT</code>
          </a>
          ) so integers decode in a single load, and reserves 2-byte length slots so messages encode
          in a single forward pass.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="lg:col-span-4">
        <div class="p-5 bg-ink text-paper border-2 border-ink rounded-md font-mono text-sm sm:text-base space-y-1.5">
          <div class="text-acid font-bold">6x faster encode</div>
          <div class="text-white font-bold">8x faster decode</div>
          <div class="text-paper/75 text-sm">1-pass write &bull; 1-load varints</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- 2. INSPECTABLE WITHOUT A SCHEMA -->
    <div class="py-8 sm:py-10 grid lg:grid-cols-12 gap-6 lg:gap-8 items-center">
      <div class="lg:col-span-8 space-y-3">
        <h3 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold uppercase tracking-tight leading-snug">
          Inspect packets without a schema &amp; use normal structs.
        </h3>
        <p class="text-base sm:text-lg text-ink/90 leading-relaxed max-w-[60ch]">
          Protobuf lumps strings, byte slices, sub-messages, maps, and packed arrays into a single
          wire type (<code>Wire Type 2</code>). Without the <code>.proto</code> file, a decoder can
          only guess what a payload contains. OmniBuf uses a 4-bit wire type (~6% larger on the
          wire) so every type is explicit. Any CLI or log viewer can decode packets without a
          schema, and you still work with normal Go structs instead of{' '}
          <a href="https://flatbuffers.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="doc-link">
            FlatBuffers
          </a>{' '}
          builders.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="lg:col-span-4">
        <div class="p-5 bg-[#FAF8F2] border-2 border-ink rounded-md font-mono text-sm sm:text-base space-y-1.5">
          <div class="font-bold text-ink">Schema-less value decoding</div>
          <div class="text-ink/80 text-sm">
            + Optional Field #0 field names for named JSON output
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- 3. READ OR SPLICE WITHOUT DECODING -->
    <div class="py-8 sm:py-10 grid lg:grid-cols-12 gap-6 lg:gap-8 items-center">
      <div class="lg:col-span-8 space-y-3">
        <h3 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold uppercase tracking-tight leading-snug">
          Read, drop, or replace individual fields on raw bytes.
        </h3>
        <p class="text-base sm:text-lg text-ink/90 leading-relaxed max-w-[60ch]">
          Because every integer and container carries its exact byte width in its header, OmniBuf
          can jump over unrelated fields in a single addition without parsing their contents.
          Generated <code>Opaque</code> views let routers and storage engines read a single nested
          field or splice updated scalar values directly into a serialized packet in ~140 ns with
          zero heap allocations.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="lg:col-span-4">
        <div class="p-5 bg-[#FAF8F2] border-2 border-ink rounded-md font-mono text-sm sm:text-base space-y-1.5">
          <div class="font-bold text-ink">Header-hop field skipping</div>
          <div class="text-ink/80 text-sm">
            <code>LocateField</code> &bull; <code>SpliceFields</code> &bull; 0 allocs
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- 4. ALL THE BENEFITS OF PROTOBUF -->
    <div class="py-8 sm:py-10 grid lg:grid-cols-12 gap-6 lg:gap-8 items-center">
      <div class="lg:col-span-8 space-y-3">
        <h3 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold uppercase tracking-tight leading-snug">
          Keep your .proto files, schema evolution, and compact payloads.
        </h3>
        <p class="text-base sm:text-lg text-ink/90 leading-relaxed max-w-[60ch]">
          OmniBuf works directly with your existing <code>.proto</code> files and generated{' '}
          <code>.pb.go</code> structs. You keep field numbers, forward/backward compatibility,
          compact payloads (40% smaller than FlatBuffers), and raw byte concatenation (
          <code>append(msgA, msgB...)</code>) for merging messages without decoding them.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="lg:col-span-4">
        <div class="p-5 bg-acid text-ink border-2 border-ink rounded-md font-mono text-sm sm:text-base font-bold space-y-1">
          <div>✓ Existing .proto schemas</div>
          <div>✓ Safe field evolution</div>
          <div>✓ append(msgA, msgB...) merge</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
